import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"ADR 0010 — one language, many interpretations (the VM is the language)","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/project/adrs/0010-one-language-many-interpretations.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/project/adrs/0010-one-language-many-interpretations.md"}`),a={name:`graphopt/project/adrs/0010-one-language-many-interpretations.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0010-—-one-language-many-interpretations-the-vm-is-the-language" tabindex="-1">ADR 0010 — one language, many interpretations (the VM <em>is</em> the language) <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0010-—-one-language-many-interpretations-the-vm-is-the-language" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR 0010 — one language, many interpretations (the VM is the language)”">​</a></h1><p><strong>Status</strong>: proposed — <strong>revised</strong> (the first draft recommended porting the pattern into Python; the revision uses <code>handl</code> <em>as</em> the kernel). <strong>A prerequisite</strong>: ADR 0009&#39;s calculus stage does not proceed until this model is fixed. <strong>Date</strong>: 2026-10-14. <strong>Depends on</strong>: ADR 0002 (categorical re-expression), ADR 0003 (four dimensions), ADR 0004 (abstraction as move), ADR 0008 (combinator presentation), ADR 0009 (the ladder and the kernel question). <strong>Prior art, verified</strong>: the <code>handl-lang/host</code> (Peregrin) workspace — <code>crates/handl-language</code> (<code>Language</code>), <code>handl-kit-arrow-lower</code>, <code>plugin-engine-{exec,typecheck,transform-rewrite,fmt,lint,test}</code>, ADR-0044 (&quot;language as data&quot;), ADR-0052 (eval schemes), ADR-0054 (type rules), ADR-0081 (data-driven child roles), ADR-0094 (generic transform engine). catopt already bridges to it: <code>catopt_discovery.handl</code>.</p><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>ADR 0009 asks whether the calculus needs a runtime VM, and its first draft implied <em>two machines</em> — a rewrite machine (apply 2-cells) and an evaluation machine (run 1-cells). That framing is wrong in a way that matters, because it implies <strong>separate languages</strong> for types, runtime and rewriting.</p><p>The <code>handl</code> platform settles the question empirically: it has <strong>one</strong> language — <code>Language</code>, whose tables are all data (<code>tokens</code>, <code>grammar</code>, <code>lexer_rules</code>, <code>eval</code> → arrow-calculus <code>Term</code> per node kind, <code>types</code> → synth/check <code>Term</code>, <code>ast</code> → shapes and named child roles, <code>transforms</code> → <code>(pass, kind, priority)</code> rules) — and <strong>many</strong> engines that read it: lexing, parsing, execution (<strong>three</strong> strategies: tree-walk, bytecode, cranelift JIT), typechecking, rewriting (<code>RewriteEngine</code> with <code>BottomUp</code>/<code>TopDown</code>/<code>Fixpoint</code> traversals), formatting, linting, testing. A language construct is a <strong>YAML fragment</strong>; only the calculus vocabulary (combinators, primitive forms, value shapes) is fixed in the engine.</p><p>So <code>eval</code>, <code>typecheck</code> and <code>rewrite</code> are not three languages. They are <strong>three interpretations of one language</strong>, differing in the scheme table they read and the globals bound at instantiation.</p><h3 id="the-mapping-to-catopt-is-nearly-1-1" tabindex="-1">The mapping to catopt is nearly 1:1 <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-mapping-to-catopt-is-nearly-1-1" aria-label="Permalink to “The mapping to catopt is nearly 1:1”">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>handl</th><th>catopt today</th><th>dimension</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Language.eval</code> (kind → arrow <code>Term</code>)</td><td>carrier/op semantics — lowering to tensor ops</td><td>semantics / execution</td></tr><tr><td><code>Language.types</code></td><td>shape rules (<code>catopt_core.typing</code>)</td><td>semantics</td></tr><tr><td><code>Language.transforms</code> (<code>TransformDef</code>)</td><td><strong>laws</strong> (<code>Rewrite</code>: lhs/rhs/cond)</td><td>semantics / search</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-engine-exec-{tree-walk,bytecode,cranelift}</code></td><td>executors (generic, batched, fused, graph, Triton)</td><td>execution</td></tr><tr><td><code>plugin-engine-transform-rewrite</code> (<code>Traversal</code>)</td><td>the <strong>e-graph</strong> — <em>one</em> rewrite <strong>strategy</strong></td><td>search</td></tr><tr><td><code>handl-kit-arrow-lower</code></td><td>the <code>lab/bridge</code> combinator prototype</td><td>semantics</td></tr><tr><td><code>merge_fragments</code> (YAML fragments → <code>Language</code>)</td><td>the law bank (currently Python)</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><p><strong>Adopt the interpretation model: one object language, many engines.</strong></p><ol><li><p><strong>One language.</strong> A single <code>Language</code> value whose tables are <strong>data</strong>: <code>semantics</code> (kind → term), <code>types</code> (kind → shape/type rule), <code>shapes</code> (node shapes + named child roles), <code>transforms</code> (laws), and the cost/executor tables. No separate language for types, runtime or rewriting.</p></li><li><p><strong>Interpretations are engines over that data.</strong> <code>eval</code> (tensor execution), <code>types</code> (shape inference), <code>rewrite</code> (laws — the e-graph is <strong>one strategy</strong>, alongside simpler passes), <code>lower</code> (to the op-graph the sinks consume), <code>cost</code>. Each takes the <code>Language</code> and its own globals; none owns the syntax.</p></li><li><p><strong>The kernel is the shared term type.</strong> The arrow-calculus <code>Term</code> (composition, <code>⊗</code>, <code>Δ</code>/<code>dup</code>, <code>loop</code>/trace, <code>arr</code>, handlers) is what schemes compile <em>into</em> — one representation the interpretations agree on. This replaces ADR 0009&#39;s <code>Machine</code> port with a <strong><code>Calculus</code></strong> (the term/rule structure) plus per-interpretation entry points.</p></li><li><p><strong>Use <code>handl</code> <em>as</em> the kernel — do not reimplement it.</strong> The first draft proposed porting the pattern into Python; that was wrong. <code>handl</code> <strong>already is</strong> the arrow calculus (<code>arr</code>, <code>flow</code>/<code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code>, <code>axis_flow</code>, <code>loop</code>, <code>if</code>, <code>op</code>/<code>handle</code>, <code>graded_arrow</code>), its language is data (ADR-0044), and it has the engines — rewrite (with traversals, traces, stats), typecheck, parse, fmt, lint, test, and <strong>three</strong> exec strategies. Critically, it <strong>supports host functions</strong> (<code>crates/handl-runtime/src/vm_primitives.rs</code> — &quot;host functions for the exec model&#39;s VM instruction set&quot;; the conformance corpus has a case calling a foreign host function), so <strong>catopt&#39;s tensor primitives are host functions</strong>, not a reason to fork the kernel.</p><p>catopt then contributes only what is genuinely catopt&#39;s: the <strong>language fragments</strong> (laws as <code>TransformDef</code> rows, shapes, semantics tables), the <strong>host functions</strong> for tensor ops, the <strong>domain adapters</strong> (<code>catopt-torch</code>/<code>onnx</code>/<code>cuda</code>) and the <strong>evaluation dimension</strong> (cost, profiles, meters), plus discovery and RL. Precedent in this repository: <code>catopt-native</code> is already an optional Rust engine, excluded from the workspace, built with maturin — a Rust kernel is an established shape here, not a new kind of dependency.</p></li><li><p><strong>Laws become rows.</strong> A law is a <code>transforms</code> entry (data), not a Python constructor — the <em>data-only</em> principle, in a format that already has a proven engine behind it. This is what gives minted laws a versioning story (ADR 0009&#39;s standing gap).</p></li></ol><h3 id="why-this-is-a-prerequisite" tabindex="-1">Why this is a prerequisite <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-this-is-a-prerequisite" aria-label="Permalink to “Why this is a prerequisite”">​</a></h3><p>ADR 0009&#39;s calculus stage cannot be scoped without it: &quot;promote the calculus to a package&quot; presumes we know whether the calculus is <em>a language with interpretations</em> (this ADR) or <em>a second representation beside the op-graph</em> (0009&#39;s original framing, which risks the drift ADR 0008 warned about). Fix the model first; then the package boundary follows.</p><h2 id="falsification" tabindex="-1">Falsification <a class="header-anchor" href="#falsification" aria-label="Permalink to “Falsification”">​</a></h2><ol start="0"><li><strong>The boundary spike decides the shape.</strong> Three unknowns, each measurable: (a) <strong>exec boundary performance</strong> — one host-function dispatch per tensor op versus catopt&#39;s batched/fused/graph paths (the kernel&#39;s job is <em>search/rewrite</em>; the hot loop is the <em>delivered</em> module, so this may not matter); (b) <strong>embedding direction</strong> — catopt embeds handl (a PyO3 peer, like <code>catopt-native</code>) or handl drives catopt as a plugin; (c) <strong>the new verification seam</strong> — term/fragment round-trip and semantics agreement, which handl&#39;s own suite cannot cover. If the spike shows the boundary cannot carry tensor semantics faithfully or efficiently, the fallback is the split: handl owns the language + rewrite + typecheck, catopt owns the tensor exec interpretation.</li><li><strong>Interpretation leakage.</strong> If the interpretations cannot share one term type without special-casing — e.g. <code>eval</code> needs a distinction <code>types</code> cannot express, or a law needs a node kind the shapes table cannot declare — the single-language claim fails and separate languages are justified. Measure: count the interpretations&#39; kind-specific special cases; the number may not grow.</li><li><strong>Data-only must not reduce expressiveness.</strong> The <strong>procedural-hook count</strong> (guards/derives the DSL cannot express — measured today: ~28, via <code>lab/audit/dspec_audit.py</code>) is a <strong>ratchet</strong>: it may only go down. If moving laws into data raises it, the data format is too weak.</li><li><strong>Performance must not regress.</strong> A generic interpretation is usually slower than a bespoke path. The existing delivery paths (generic/batched/fused/graph/Triton) must stay at least as fast; a generic interpreter is <em>additional</em>, not a replacement, until measured.</li><li><strong>No interop drift.</strong> If catopt&#39;s <code>Language</code> fragments and handl&#39;s diverge — the bridge stops round-tripping — the seam is worse than no seam.</li></ol><h2 id="honest-scars" tabindex="-1">Honest scars <a class="header-anchor" href="#honest-scars" aria-label="Permalink to “Honest scars”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong><code>handl</code> is Rust + YAML with its own crate graph</strong>, so using it as the kernel means a compiled dependency and a second toolchain. It cannot live <em>inside</em> <code>catopt-core</code> (zero-dependency, <code>import-linter</code>-enforced); it is a <strong>peer</strong> — exactly the shape <code>catopt-native</code> already has.</li><li><strong>The first draft was wrong</strong> and is recorded rather than quietly dropped: porting the pattern into Python would have duplicated a proven engine and created the drift this ADR&#39;s own falsification forbids.</li><li><strong>The current e-graph is op-graph-specific.</strong> Calling it &quot;one rewrite strategy&quot; is the <em>direction</em>; today it is the only one, and it is bound to the op-graph IR.</li><li><strong>Two representations still coexist</strong> (op-graph IR + calculus term) until the canonical-representation decision lands — this ADR fixes the <em>model</em>, not that choice.</li><li><strong>The data format is unproven at bank scale.</strong> 82 rules as rows is plausible; the guards/derives are where it will be tested.</li></ul><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>The &quot;laws are exclusively data&quot; rule becomes implementable</strong> — with a ratchet (the procedural-hook count) as the honest form of &quot;exclusively&quot;.</li><li><strong>The e-graph stops being the architecture.</strong> It becomes one search strategy behind a <code>rewrite</code> interpretation, so simpler passes and future strategies are peers.</li><li><strong>New semantics = new schemes.</strong> Extending the vocabulary is a data change (plus, where genuinely new, a handler), which is what makes &quot;the machine can create new semantics&quot; a <em>mechanism</em> rather than a hope.</li><li><strong>catopt and handl share one idea</strong>, so a construct expressed in one has a target in the other — via the existing bridge.</li></ul><h2 id="outcome-spike-2026-10-14" tabindex="-1">Outcome (spike, 2026-10-14) <a class="header-anchor" href="#outcome-spike-2026-10-14" aria-label="Permalink to “Outcome (spike, 2026-10-14)”">​</a></h2><p>The boundary spike (<code>plan 0029</code>) measured this ADR&#39;s central claim and <strong>it holds</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>The load works.</strong> <code>language_from_dir</code> assembles a <code>Language</code> from a directory of catopt-emitted fragments, and after closing two gaps the <strong>whole bank loads — 83 laws</strong> in <code>lang.transforms</code>, with the three minted leaf kinds (<code>catopt:const</code> / <code>catopt:var</code> / <code>catopt:data</code>) declared (<code>handl 9372bd32</code>, catopt <code>5ca8aef</code>).</li><li><strong>The two gaps were representational, not semantic</strong>: handl&#39;s <code>when</code> is ONE condition (fold the clause list into <code>And([...])</code>), and enum variants are YAML <em>tags</em> under <code>serde_yaml</code>, not JSON maps. A third — the <code>pass:node_kind:priority</code> rule identity — required a load-time disambiguator, since catopt&#39;s saturation has no order to give.</li><li><strong>The tensor channel exists but is not needed</strong>: <code>TaggedValue::Resource</code> carries an opaque host pointer by reference, while the <em>host-function seam</em> is JSON-valued, so a tensor would cross as a debug process-local pointer or as copied bytes. ADR 0011 keeps tensors out of v1 entirely, which is why this is a footnote rather than a blocker.</li><li><strong>Direction</strong>: (A) — catopt embeds handl as an optional peer, the <code>catopt-native</code> pattern (<code>retros/handl-q3-integration-direction.md</code>).</li></ul><p>What this ADR still does <strong>not</strong> have: a <em>built</em> peer, or a rewrite run through handl&#39;s engine on a catopt term. Those are sequenced in <code>plans/0030-handl-peer-integration.md</code>.</p>`,23)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};